(TaskRunner pid=2882) - \( 17_b \) in base 10 is \( 1 \times b + 7 = b + 7 \).
(TaskRunner pid=2882) - \( 97_b \) in base 10 is \( 9 \times b + 7 = 9b + 7 \).
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) We need \( b + 7 \) to divide \( 9b + 7 \). This can be written as:
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \[ 9b + 7 = k \times (b + 7) \]
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) where \( k \) is an integer. Rearranging and solving for \( b \):
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \[ 9b + 7 = kb + 7k \]
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \[ (9 - k)b + (7 - 7k) = 0 \]
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \[ (9 - k)b = 7k - 7 \]
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \[ b = \frac{7(k - 1)}{9 - k} \]
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) We can also perform polynomial division of \( 9b + 7 \) by \( b + 7 \):
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \[ 9b + 7 = 9(b + 7) - 56 \]
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) This implies that \( b + 7 \) must be a divisor of 56. The positive divisors of 56 are 1, 2, 4, 7, 8, 14, 28, and 56. Since \( b > 9 \), \( b + 7 \) must be greater than 16. The relevant divisors are 28 and 56.
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) Thus, we have:
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) - \( b + 7 = 28 \) gives \( b = 21 \)
(TaskRunner pid=2882) - \( b + 7 = 56 \) gives \( b = 49 \)
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) Both 21 and 49 are valid bases. Verifying these:
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) - For \( b = 21 \), \( 17_{21} = 28 \) and \( 97_{21} = 196 \), and 28 divides 196.
(TaskRunner pid=2882) - For \( b = 49 \), \( 17_{49} = 56 \) and \( 97_{49} = 448 \), and 56 divides 448.
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) Therefore, the sum of all such bases is \( 21 + 49 = 70 \).
(TaskRunner pid=2882) 
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \[
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \boxed{70}
(TaskRunner pid=2882) \]
